  "intro": [
    {
      "title": "Jak było? — reacting to anything that finished",
      "title_pl": "Jak było?",
      "body": "Polish small talk runs on a small set of fixed phrases. Learn each one whole — do not build them word by word, and do not translate them piece by piece.\n\nYou already have było (\"it was\"). Put a describing word after it and you can react to anything that is over: a weekend, a film, a day at work.\n\nOne thing to notice: the word after było is an adverb — dobrze, fajnie — not the adjective dobra / dobre / dobry. An adjective describes a noun (kawa była dobra); an adverb describes how something went (było dobrze). Super never changes at all.",
      "body_pl": "Jak było? Było super. Było fajnie. Było dobrze.",
      "table": {
        "headers": [
          "Polish",
          "English",
          "When you use it"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Jak było?",
            "How was it?",
            "asking about anything that has finished"
          ],
          [
            "Było super.",
            "It was great.",
            "enthusiastic"
          ],
          [
            "Było fajnie.",
            "It was nice.",
            "warm, everyday"
          ],
          [
            "Było dobrze.",
            "It was good.",
            "neutral, safe"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "How was it at work?",
          "pl": "Jak było w pracy?"
        },
        {
          "en": "How was it at school?",
          "pl": "Jak było w szkole?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Co słychać? — the standing greeting",
      "title_pl": "Co słychać?",
      "body": "Where English says \"How's it going?\", Polish says Co słychać? There is nothing useful to take apart here, so take it whole.\n\nThree safe answers. Wszystko means \"everything\". W porządku you already know as \"fine\". Nic means \"nothing\" — and after nic, Polish uses the genitive, which is why new comes out as nowego rather than nowy. Learn the pair Nic nowego as one answer.",
      "body_pl": "Co słychać? — Wszystko dobrze. / Wszystko w porządku. / Nic nowego.",
      "table": {
        "headers": [
          "Polish",
          "English",
          "New word in it"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Co słychać?",
            "How's it going?",
            "słychać"
          ],
          [
            "Wszystko dobrze.",
            "Everything's good.",
            "wszystko = everything"
          ],
          [
            "Wszystko w porządku.",
            "Everything's fine.",
            "w porządku — already known"
          ],
          [
            "Nic nowego.",
            "Nothing new.",
            "nic = nothing"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "Hi! How's it going?",
          "pl": "Cześć! Co słychać?"
        },
        {
          "en": "Everything's fine, thank you.",
          "pl": "Wszystko w porządku, dziękuję."
        },
        {
          "en": "Nothing new, thanks.",
          "pl": "Nic nowego, dzięki."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Asking about the weekend and about yesterday",
      "title_pl": "Weekend i wczoraj",
      "body": "Two ready-made openers that sit on the past tense you have just learned.\n\nJak było w weekend? — \"How was the weekend?\" You already know every piece: Jak było? plus w weekend. The same trick works for any place you know: Jak było w pracy?\n\nCo robiłeś wczoraj? / Co robiłaś wczoraj? — here too you know every word. Choose robiłeś when you are speaking to a man, robiłaś when you are speaking to a woman, exactly as with byłeś / byłaś.",
      "body_pl": "Jak było w weekend? Co robiłeś / robiłaś wczoraj?",
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "Hello. How was the weekend?",
          "pl": "Dzień dobry. Jak było w weekend?"
        },
        {
          "en": "What were you doing yesterday? (to a man)",
          "pl": "Co robiłeś wczoraj?"
        },
        {
          "en": "What were you doing yesterday? (to a woman)",
          "pl": "Co robiłaś wczoraj?"
        },
        {
          "en": "The weekend was great.",
          "pl": "Weekend był super."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
